@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "INSERT Operation";
}

<section>

    <!-- PAGE INTRODUCTION -->

    <div class="mb-5">

        <h1 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            INSERT Operation
        </h1>

        <p class="lead text-muted">
            The INSERT statement is used to add new records into a
            SQL Server table.
        </p>

        <p>
            INSERT is one of the most commonly used SQL commands.
            Whenever we want to store a new student, employee,
            customer, product or any other record in a table,
            we use the INSERT statement.
        </p>

    </div>


    <!-- WHAT IS INSERT -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            What is INSERT?
        </h2>

        <p>
            The <strong>INSERT</strong> statement adds one or more
            new rows to an existing table.
        </p>

        <p>
            The values supplied in the INSERT statement are stored
            in the corresponding columns of the table.
        </p>

    </section>


    <!-- SYNTAX -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            INSERT Syntax
        </h2>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>INSERT INTO table_name
(
    column1,
    column2,
    column3
)
VALUES
(
    value1,
    value2,
    value3
);</code></pre>

        </div>

        <p class="mt-3">
            Here, the column names specify where the values should
            be stored, while the VALUES clause provides the actual
            data.
        </p>

    </section>


    <!-- SAMPLE TABLE -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Example Table
        </h2>

        <p>
            We will use the following Students table for our examples.
        </p>

        <div class="table-responsive">

            <table class="table table-bordered table-striped">

                <thead class="table-dark">

                    <tr>
                        <th>StudentId</th>
                        <th>StudentName</th>
                        <th>Age</th>
                        <th>City</th>
                    </tr>

                </thead>

                <tbody>

                    <tr>
                        <td>101</td>
                        <td>Rahul</td>
                        <td>22</td>
                        <td>Pune</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>102</td>
                        <td>Priya</td>
                        <td>23</td>
                        <td>Mumbai</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>103</td>
                        <td>Amit</td>
                        <td>21</td>
                        <td>Nashik</td>
                    </tr>

                </tbody>

            </table>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- INSERT SINGLE RECORD -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Insert a Single Record
        </h2>

        <p>
            We can insert a single student record using the following
            INSERT statement.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>INSERT INTO Students
(
    StudentId,
    StudentName,
    Age,
    City
)
VALUES
(
    104,
    'Neha',
    22,
    'Pune'
);</code></pre>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- OUTPUT -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Output
        </h2>

        <p>
            After executing the INSERT statement, the new record is
            added to the Students table.
        </p>

        <div class="table-responsive">

            <table class="table table-bordered table-striped">

                <thead class="table-dark">

                    <tr>
                        <th>StudentId</th>
                        <th>StudentName</th>
                        <th>Age</th>
                        <th>City</th>
                    </tr>

                </thead>

                <tbody>

                    <tr>
                        <td>101</td>
                        <td>Rahul</td>
                        <td>22</td>
                        <td>Pune</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>102</td>
                        <td>Priya</td>
                        <td>23</td>
                        <td>Mumbai</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>103</td>
                        <td>Amit</td>
                        <td>21</td>
                        <td>Nashik</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>104</td>
                        <td>Neha</td>
                        <td>22</td>
                        <td>Pune</td>
                    </tr>

                </tbody>

            </table>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- INSERT WITHOUT COLUMN NAMES -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            INSERT Without Specifying Column Names
        </h2>

        <p>
            If values are supplied for every column in the exact
            table-column order, column names can be omitted.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>INSERT INTO Students
VALUES
(
    105,
    'Kiran',
    24,
    'Mumbai'
);</code></pre>

        </div>

        <div class="alert alert-warning border-0 mt-4">

            <strong>Important:</strong>
            When column names are omitted, the values must follow
            the exact order of the table columns.

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- INSERT MULTIPLE RECORDS -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Insert Multiple Records
        </h2>

        <p>
            SQL Server allows multiple rows to be inserted using
            a single INSERT statement.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>INSERT INTO Students
(
    StudentId,
    StudentName,
    Age,
    City
)
VALUES
(106, 'Sneha', 22, 'Pune'),
(107, 'Aakash', 23, 'Mumbai'),
(108, 'Pooja', 21, 'Nashik');</code></pre>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- INSERT SPECIFIC COLUMNS -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Insert Data into Specific Columns
        </h2>

        <p>
            We do not always need to provide values for every column.
            We can specify only the columns for which we want to
            insert values, provided the remaining columns allow
            omitted values.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>INSERT INTO Students
(
    StudentId,
    StudentName,
    Age
)
VALUES
(
    109,
    'Riya',
    22
);</code></pre>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- STRING VALUES -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            String Values in INSERT
        </h2>

        <p>
            Character and string values are generally written inside
            single quotation marks.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>INSERT INTO Students
(
    StudentId,
    StudentName,
    Age,
    City
)
VALUES
(
    110,
    'Rohan',
    24,
    'Pune'
);</code></pre>

        </div>

        <p class="mt-3">
            Numeric values such as StudentId and Age are written
            without quotation marks, while text values such as
            StudentName and City are written inside single quotes.
        </p>

    </section>


    <!-- INSERT NULL -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            INSERT with NULL
        </h2>

        <p>
            If a column allows NULL values, NULL can be inserted when
            no value is available.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>INSERT INTO Students
(
    StudentId,
    StudentName,
    Age,
    City
)
VALUES
(
    111,
    'Vikas',
    NULL,
    'Pune'
);</code></pre>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- PRACTICAL EXAMPLE -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Practical Example
        </h2>

        <p>
            Consider a Student Management System. When a new student
            takes admission, the application can store the student's
            information in the Students table.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>INSERT INTO Students
(
    StudentId,
    StudentName,
    Age,
    City
)
VALUES
(
    112,
    'Snehal',
    23,
    'Pune'
);</code></pre>

        </div>

        <p class="mt-3">
            After execution, a new row containing Snehal's information
            is added to the Students table.
        </p>

    </section>


    <!-- IMPORTANT POINTS -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <div class="alert alert-info border-0">

            <h4 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Important Points
            </h4>

            <ul class="mb-0">

                <li class="mb-2">
                    INSERT is used to add new records.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    The number of values should match the specified
                    columns.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    String values are written inside single quotes.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Multiple rows can be inserted using one INSERT
                    statement.
                </li>

                <li>
                    Column names should be specified whenever possible
                    for clarity and maintainability.
                </li>

            </ul>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- SUMMARY -->

    <section class="mb-4">

        <div class="alert alert-primary border-0">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Summary
            </h2>

            <p class="mb-0">
                In this section, we learned how the INSERT statement
                is used to add records to a SQL Server table. We
                covered inserting a single record, inserting multiple
                records, inserting into specific columns, using string
                values and inserting NULL values.
            </p>

        </div>

    </section>


